@model ShowcaseModel
@{ ViewData["Title"] = "Filters matrix"; }
<h1>See which input changes a decision.</h1><p class="lead">Every row runs its actual published .NET filter. Presets change this request's principal, browser headers, country and Order together.</p>
<p><a class="button" href="/filters?preset=matching">Matching preset</a> <a class="button" href="/filters?preset=nonmatching">Non-matching preset</a></p>
<section><h2>Inputs supplied before evaluation</h2><dl><dt>Identity / claim</dt><dd>@Model.Persona.Name / role=@Model.Persona.Role</dd><dt>Country / language</dt><dd>@Model.Persona.Country / @Model.Persona.Language</dd><dt>User-Agent</dt><dd class="wrap">@Model.Persona.Agent</dd><dt>Order</dt><dd>@Model.Persona.Order.Id · Vip=@Model.Persona.Order.Vip</dd></dl></section>
<section><h2>Eleven-filter matrix</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Flag</th><th>Actual result</th><th>What to expect</th></tr></thead><tbody>
@foreach (var key in FeatureFlags.Filters) { <tr><td><code>@key</code></td><td class="@(Model.Flags[key] ? "on" : "off")">@(Model.Flags[key] ? "ON" : "OFF")</td><td>@(key == "filter-device-type" ? "Native parser: Mac; shared Macintosh rule does not match" : key == "filter-percentage" ? "50% sticky; either result is valid" : key == "filter-always-on" ? "ON for both presets" : key == "filter-time-window" ? "ON while 2020–2099 window is open" : "Matching ON; Non-matching OFF")</td></tr> }
</tbody></table><p>The shared DeviceType rule is <code>Macintosh</code>, but the published .NET UA parser reports <code>Mac</code> for the Matching UA. This row stays OFF in both presets; it is an SDK parser/template parity gap. OperatingSystem matches <code>Mac</code>. A browser name and a device family are different inputs.</p>
<p>Local .NET aliases are <code>CountryFamily</code> for Country and <code>OS</code> for OperatingSystem. The dashboard uses the friendly filter names in the shared template. The claims/browser segment filters use Percentage=100 so only the segment determines the outcome.</p></section>
